    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Hearing-impaired individuals with cochlear implants (CIs) often face difficulty accurately reporting what they hear. Consequently, clinicians require objective tools to differentiate whether auditory deficits in CI patients arise from inadequate signal delivery from their implants or from difficulty with assigning perceptual labels. One objective tool to evaluate speech perception involves applying classification—a machine-learning technique—to the frequency following response (FFR), an evoked potential of the auditory brainstem recorded with electroencephalography. While classification has been successful evaluating FFRs to normal sounds, it is unknown if this approach is valid in conditions where signals are degraded in a way that simulates CIs. We assessed perception of 13 normal hearing participants to six vowel stimuli in two conditions: 1) intact condition with unaltered stimuli, and 2) vocoded condition, where stimuli were processed through an acoustic simulation of a CI. Participants completed a vowel identification task, achieving an average accuracy of 92.81 % in the intact condition. As expected, participants performed worse in the vocoded condition, achieving an accuracy of 77.25 %. FFR data was classified to reveal high accuracy (73.08 %, p < 0.001) in the intact condition. Notably, classification was more accurate (85.13 %, p < 0.001) in the vocoded condition, suggesting that CI simulations may enhance features of the vowels that contribute to accurate classification. These findings imply that classification is feasible for objectively assessing perception of degraded signals. Thus, classification remains a potentially useful tool for directly evaluating speech perception of CI patients. Further investigation is required to determine how perceptual responses relate to neural features relevant to the classification model.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
